A self-hosted alternative to Google Photos and iCloud
Off The Cloud is free, open-source software that turns a Raspberry Pi 5 into a home server for your family's photos, videos and files: it does the job of Google Photos or iCloud Photos, on a small device you own. Your phones back up your photos and videos in full quality, and the device tags them for search and keeps them on two mirrored drives, with no monthly storage plan. It suits families who would rather own a small device than pay for cloud storage every month.
@a.href=https://demo.off-the.cloud/
Try the demo Look around a real device with a made-up family's photos and files. No sign-up.
What you keep from Google Photos and iCloud
Automatic backup from your phones. The iPhone and Android apps back up your photos and videos in the background, in full resolution, including iPhone photos kept only in iCloud.
Your originals, untouched. Each photo and video is stored as it was taken, never compressed or resized to save space.
Search and faces. Search for "beach", "dog" or a city, and browse by person and by date. Tagging, place names and face recognition all work on the device itself, and face recognition stays off until you turn it on.
Room for the whole family. Everyone gets their own account and password on the same device, and their photos are encrypted with their own key.
Sharing. Send a collection to anyone as a link that expires: they open it in a browser, with no account or app. An optional private feed lets you share posts with friends you accept, with no ads or algorithm.
What changes when you leave the cloud
You buy the hardware once. A Raspberry Pi 5 (8 GB) and two USB pen drives are all it needs. When they fill up, you swap in bigger drives instead of paying for a bigger plan.
You set it up, once. The Mac, Windows or Linux app prepares the system card, and the setup finishes over Bluetooth from your phone, or from the Mac app: Wi-Fi, a name and a password. It takes about 20 minutes, with no terminal.
Away from home, you need a way in. At home, the apps talk to the device directly. Elsewhere they go through our optional relay, the bridge, which is free for the first two years and then costs €9.99 + VAT a year for up to five addresses. Without it, a VPN into your home network still lets you reach your photos, and so does Tailscale Funnel for a device only you use; friends, posts, share links that work from anywhere and phone notifications need the bridge.
You take ownership. Your photos are on two mirrored drives in your home, so one drive failing loses nothing, and a full backup is easy: take one drive out and put a new one in, and the device copies everything onto it; the drive you took out is your backup. Nobody can reset your device password, not even us.
