Which one should you choose?
Off The Cloud if you want your family's photos and files at home, in full quality, with no storage plan to outgrow, on a small device that's easy to set up and needs almost no technical knowledge.
Google Photos if you want nothing to set up or look after, and don't mind Google keeping your photos or paying for a plan once the free 15 GB fills up.
iCloud Photos if everyone in your family uses an iPhone or a Mac: it's built in, and with Advanced Data Protection turned on (not offered in the UK), your photo library is end-to-end encrypted.
Immich if you already run a home server and are comfortable with Docker: it's a mature, free and open-source photo app.
Synology Photos if you want a ready-made NAS from an established brand that does much more than photos, and don't need the software to be open source.
Ente if you'd rather keep your photos in a cloud where not even the provider can see them: it's end-to-end encrypted and open source, and paid plans keep three copies in the EU.
Moving your photos from iCloud or Google Photos
From iCloud Photos: install the iPhone app and, when it asks which photos to send, choose All photos and videos and leave Sync from iCloud on. The phone downloads each photo it keeps only in iCloud and sends it to your device, so a large library takes a while; keep the phone on Wi-Fi. The device gets each photo's original: edits and the moving part of Live Photos stay in iCloud, and albums aren't copied. Backing up doesn't remove anything from iCloud.
From Google Photos: Off The Cloud has no Google Photos importer. The Android and iPhone apps back up the photos and videos stored on the phone, so whatever Google Photos still keeps there comes across, but photos that exist only in Google's cloud have to be downloaded to a phone or computer first.
From a computer: the Mac, Windows and Linux apps back up any folder you choose, so photos you keep on a computer come across too.
