FAQ
Questions people actually ask
Can I try it without leaving Google Photos or iCloud?
@a.href=https://demo.off-the.cloud/
Yes. The apps copy your photos to the device rather than moving them, so Google Photos and iCloud carry on as before and you can use both until you're sure. You can also look around the demo first: a real device with a made-up family's photos, no sign-up needed.
Is it cheaper than Google One or iCloud+?
It depends on how much you keep. Over five years, at October 2026 prices, iCloud+ costs €179.40 for 200 GB and €599.40 for 2 TB, and Google One's 2 TB plan €499.95 paid yearly. With Off The Cloud you buy a Raspberry Pi 5 and two USB drives once and, if you use the bridge, pay €29.97 + VAT for its third to fifth years; the device also uses some electricity. When the drives fill up, you buy bigger ones instead of a bigger plan.
Is Off The Cloud end-to-end encrypted?
No. Your files are stored encrypted on your Off The Cloud device, with a key only your password unlocks, and at home the apps connect to it directly. Away from home, connections go through the bridge, which decrypts and re-encrypts them as they pass, so they are not end-to-end encrypted; nothing that passes through is stored. File names, tags and the photos you post for friends are kept readable, so search and the feed work. If you need end-to-end encryption, Ente has it, and so does iCloud with Advanced Data Protection turned on, except in the UK.
How is it different from Immich?
Immich is a free, open-source photo app for people who run their own server: you install it with Docker on a Linux machine and set up storage, backups and remote access yourself. Off The Cloud needs almost no technical knowledge: the app prepares a Raspberry Pi's card and the setup finishes over Bluetooth from your phone or Mac, on inexpensive hardware, a Raspberry Pi 5 and two USB pen drives. It also does more than photos: your files stay in sync with your computers, like a NAS, and a private social network connects friends' devices, which Immich doesn't have.
What happens if a drive or the Raspberry Pi breaks?
If a drive fails, the other one holds a full copy: the device keeps working, tells you which drive to replace and fills the new one by itself. If the Raspberry Pi dies, replace it: everything is on the drives, so moving the card and the drives to a new Pi brings it back as it was. For a full backup, take one drive out and put a new one in: the drive you took out is your backup.
